In a society ordered by rank and role, the engineers hold a designated station of technical authority. They have been appointed and trained to make precisely these judgments, and their unanimous recommendation carries the weight of their office. The residents, though numerous, are stepping outside their proper role when they substitute unverified online opinion for the settled expertise of those charged with the duty of safeguarding the water. Order is preserved when each defers to the authority proper to the matter at hand.

Yet social order is not only hierarchy—it is also harmony. Authority that ignores the people it serves breeds distrust and disorder. A wise official does not merely command; he instructs those below so that they willingly accept what is right. Thus the engineers' judgment should prevail, but the proper course is for those in authority to educate the residents, restoring both correct decision and social concord. The residents' mere preference, formed against expert counsel, does not itself constitute a legitimate ground to overturn those whose station is to know.

