In a society ordered by rank and role, a neighborhood carries an established character, and its longtime residents hold a rightful standing that a newcomer enters into, not above. A business does not exist in isolation from the community that sustains it; it takes its place within an existing order and owes deference to the customs already settled there. The market-logic view, which treats commerce as answerable only to individual choice, dissolves the very bonds of role and obligation that hold society together. That said, this is a matter of custom and respect, not health or safety, so the proper remedy is deference and harmony rather than force—dialogue that leads the newcomer to honor the established ways is fitting.

The residents' standing as those rooted in the neighborhood gives their concerns real moral authority. The restaurant, as the newcomer, owes them respect. Pure market reasoning carries little weight here because it denies the social bonds that ground moral life.

