Reasoning from within a society ordered by rank and role: the central concern is whether social order and the proper relations between stations are honored or degraded. A wealthy man of high station has obligations that come with his rank—chief among them the duty to uphold the dignity of those beneath him, not to exploit his position to degrade them for amusement. Even with consent and payment, turning the humiliation of lower-ranked people into entertainment corrupts the bond of superior and inferior that holds society together. The superior's role is to protect and dignify, not to debase. Consent does not license this, because the whole point of a rank-ordered morality is that station carries duties that cannot be waived by mutual agreement.

The power imbalance matters greatly here, because in a hierarchical order the dependence of the low upon the high is precisely why the high bear responsibility. Their "voluntary" participation flows from need, and the man abuses his standing. Individual free choice carries little weight against the corrosion of the proper ordering of persons—it is not simply a private transaction but a public wound to the dignity of a class of people, however hidden.

