In a society ordered by rank and role, one's conduct is a reflection of one's station and of the authority one represents. Even in private, a person of standing carries the dignity of their role, and speech that degrades a vulnerable group—those whom the higher ranks are duty-bound to protect and shelter—is a lapse in the character proper to one's position. The friend's discomfort reflects a rightly-ordered conscience sensing that the harmony and hierarchy of proper regard was violated. Yet since no member of the group was harmed and this was a private lapse rather than a public breach of order, it weighs more as a defect of character than as a grave disruption of the social fabric.

The claim that private speech falls outside the moral domain sits poorly with this worldview: character and station govern all conduct, public and private, because superiors owe protection downward and all owe respect to the order of ranks. Context does matter—private jest is less injurious to public order than public contempt—but it does not erase the fault.

