In a society ordered by rank and role, wealth is not merely a private possession but a position that carries obligations to those below and around you. The village produced the conditions — the shared labor, the customs, the social fabric — that made his success possible. His station now entails duties: hospitality, contribution to collective works, care for those in need. To withdraw from communal life while enjoying visible advantage is to abandon the reciprocal obligations that hold the social order together. The harm here is not fraud but the corrosion of the bonds that give everyone their place.

That said, since no one was cheated, the offense is one of neglected duty rather than active wrong. The purely individualist view — that his property is nobody's business — is largely foreign to this worldview, since it denies that station generates obligation at all. The "selfish but not immoral" position captures something (there is no crime), but from this perspective persistent neglect of communal duty is precisely a moral failing, not merely bad taste.

