In a society organized around rank and role, obligations flow from one's station and from the bonds established over a lifetime of service. This elder held a role of genuine authority and function—mediator, mentor, contributor to communal work. Those roles created lasting obligations that do not simply dissolve when a person can no longer produce. The younger members who benefited from his mentorship stand in a relation of debt and deference to him. To abandon him would invert the proper order: the community would be treating role and rank as merely transactional, dissolving the very hierarchy of obligation that gives the society its structure.

The practical concern about resources is not worthless—a well-ordered community must sustain itself and cannot pretend scarcity away. But in this frame, honoring one's obligations to those who occupied roles of service is close to the heart of what social order means. The elder's past station continues to command respect. So the strongest reading is that care is owed, while prudent stewardship of resources is a subordinate consideration to be managed, not a trump card.

