In our ordering, the eldest brother holds a place that the family has long honored, and this is no small thing. The bonds between siblings, and the harmony of the house, rest partly on the respect owed to seniority. Yet role is not only about rank—it is also about the proper use of what one has been given. A person of real talent who refuses to serve fully wastes what Heaven and cultivation have granted, and this too is a failure of one's station. The wisest path honors both: she takes up the work she is fitted for, while carrying herself so that her brother is not shamed. To advance is not the fault; to advance carelessly, forgetting the one above you, is the fault. The refinement of the superior person lies exactly in walking this line—rising without trampling, succeeding while yielding honor upward.

Pure refusal (A in judgment) sacrifices legitimate role-fulfillment and treats harmony as mere stasis. Pure self-assertion (B) forgets that no talent exists apart from the relationships that formed it. The balanced course carries the most weight.

