In a society ordered by rank and role, the father's station carries clear obligations. A father is responsible for the formation of his son's character and for maintaining right relations with his neighbor. The wrong done to the neighbor's property is a breach of order that the father, as head of his household, bears responsibility for correcting. Letting the son evade accountability because others go unpunished treats justice as a matter of individual calculation rather than of one's duty to those above and around him. The father owes it to the neighbor (a fellow member of the community), to his son (whose character he must shape), and to the social fabric to insist on confession.

The notion that moral agency must be self-generated and cannot be imposed sits uneasily with a worldview where a child's proper development comes precisely through being guided by rightful authority. A father who abdicates this is failing his role. Repairing the harm matters, but in this framework accountability and the restoration of right relations are inseparable from how it is done—a hidden restitution would not restore the son's standing or teach him his obligations.

