In a society ordered by rank and role, a person's identity is not self-authored but received. The young man was born into a fishing community whose way of life, obligations, and shared understanding of who its people are constitute the moral fabric he inherited. To redefine himself as an artist, even while remaining physically present, is to withdraw from the shared self-understanding that binds the community. His station carries duties and a place in the order; to "define himself differently" is precisely to place his own conception of self above the community's authority to name him.

Yet from within this same frame, staying and continuing to contribute matters. If art could be understood as a role within the community's order—a craftsman serving communal purposes rather than an individualist declaring independence—the tension would soften. The trouble is that his framing is self-definition against the community's definition, which the ordered view cannot fully endorse. The claim of pure individual self-construction (B) carries almost no weight here; it denies the very foundation of moral life.

