In a society ordered by rank and role, the man occupies two stations at once: he is the eldest son with a duty to his parents and the ancestral line, and he is a father and husband with duties to those beneath his care. The weight of five generations is real—continuity of the family craft is a genuine obligation sanctioned by his father's authority and the ancestral order. Yet the ordering of roles also recognizes that a father's duty downward to his dependents is itself part of the social fabric; children thriving and a wife's established position are not mere preferences but the man's own household, which he is charged to govern well.

The dominant frame here privileges the vertical obligation upward and the continuity of the line—these carry the heaviest formal weight in a rank-and-role society. But the duty to one's own household is also role-grounded, not a matter of individual whim. The purely individualist option (each must decide alone, no tradition can guide) sits poorly with this worldview, since the whole point is that stations, not private conscience, define obligation.

