In a society ordered by rank and role, distribution is not a matter of abstract need or equality, but of proper obligation flowing through established relationships. The organization has a defined position within the community, and its duties run first to those under its charge—those it is responsible for and connected to by role. Authority and station determine who one is bound to serve. Yet even within this framework, a properly ordered society recognizes that those who are most vulnerable and unable to fend for themselves have a rightful claim on those above them; protecting the weak is part of the duty of station. Pure impartial equality carries little weight here, since moral life is defined by the specific bonds of place and role rather than by treating all as interchangeable.

So the primary weight falls on obligations arising from relationship and standing, with meaningful secondary weight given to shielding the vulnerable, which is itself a duty of one's position.

