In a society ordered by rank and role, kinship is not a matter of personal acquaintance but of standing within a web of obligations that precedes any individual choice. Blood ties carry weight regardless of whether one has met the relative, because the family as an institution is a moral unit, and its members hold duties to one another by virtue of their station. The safety net of mutual family support is precisely what keeps the social order intact — when a relative falters, the family's proper role is to sustain them, for this is how the whole structure endures across generations.

That said, obligations in such a society are also calibrated by degree of relation and one's own capacity. A distant relative sits at the edge of the web, so the duty is real but not equal to that owed to close kin. The idea that one owes nothing to someone unmet (B) contradicts the very foundation of role-based morality, which does not depend on personal sentiment. The notion that sacrifice shouldn't be "expected" (C) reflects a more individualist ethic that sits uneasily with this worldview, though it captures the reasonable sense that burden should track proximity and means.

