In a society ordered by rank and role, the teacher's duty flows from her appointed station. Her obligation is not primarily to maximize aggregate benefit (a merchant's calculus) nor to indulge personal favoritism, but to fulfill the responsibility entrusted to her by the authority above her—the school, the community. A teacher's role is to serve all her students according to the proper functioning of her office. Yet there is also a recognized duty of the strong toward those most in need under one's charge; a superior owes protection and care to the weakest dependent. Both the aim of serving the whole class faithfully and the special duty to the one in gravest need carry real weight, while decisions based merely on personal closeness undermine the impartiality proper to her station.

The tension is between fulfilling her office toward the collective (spreading help, sustaining the general order) and answering the deepest need placed within her charge. Personal relationship is the least fitting basis, as it corrupts the fairness her role demands.

