In a society ordered by rank and role, family traditions are not merely private preferences—they are the visible expression of one's place within the family and the honoring of those above (parents, elders, ancestors). Participation affirms the hierarchy and continuity that hold the family together. Refusal, even when it harms no one directly, signals a withdrawal from one's proper station and a failure of the deference owed to those who lead the family. The feelings of disrespect the family reports are not incidental; they register a real breach in the fabric of social order.

That said, the tradition-bound view still recognizes that the refusal causes no material harm, and a wise elder might see that respect can sometimes be shown through other forms of dutiful conduct. But the emphasis on personal choice as the measure of meaning is largely foreign to this worldview—obligations flow from one's role, not from individual election.

