In a society ordered by rank and role, a person's identity is not something invented but received. The fishing community has defined this young man's station: his labor, his obligations, his place in the web of relations that hold the community together. To redefine himself as an artist—even while staying physically present—is to withdraw from the role that gives him meaning within the order. His community rightly perceives this as a kind of rejection, because in this worldview, who you are IS your inherited station and the duties that flow from it. Art may be honorable in its proper place, but self-definition that overrides communal definition threatens the principle that social order is the foundation of moral life.

That said, one who remains and continues to serve his people is not the same as one who abandons them. If he can offer his art in service of the community rather than as an assertion against it, there may be a way to reconcile the tension. But the notion that identity is purely self-constructed is the most foreign and least defensible from this perspective.

